srt0 — Sysl runtime crt0
========================

The arch-specific `_start` (in `prog_start.s` on x86_64, in
`syscall.asm` on TRISC) reads argc/argv from the POSIX blob at
`PROG_ARGS_ADDR` and calls [[sysl_start]]. This module walks the
C-style argv, wraps each entry as a sysl `string`, and invokes the
program's `main(argv: []string)`. When main returns, srt0 exits
via syscall.

C programs link against `crt0.c` instead — same ABI on the inside
(`oskit_ulib__sysl_start`), but forwards argc/argv straight to a
C `int main(int argc, char **argv)` without the `[]string` wrap.

Layout at `PROG_ARGS_ADDR` (written by PM during spawn): 4-byte
argc at offset 0, 4 zero padding, then an array of argc 8-byte
absolute pointers (baked by the sender since `PROG_ARGS_ADDR` is
fixed across processes), followed by an 8-byte NULL terminator
and the NUL-separated string data that each argv entry points at.

    module oskit.ulib

    val ARG_MAX_ARGS = 64
    var prog_argv_strings: [64]string

    extern main(argv: []string)

    sysl_start(argc: int, cargv: **byte)
        var i = 0
        while i < argc
            if i >= ARG_MAX_ARGS
                break
            val p = cargv[i]
            var n = 0
            while p[n] != 0
                n += 1
            prog_argv_strings[i] = string(p, n)
            i += 1
        main(prog_argv_strings[:i])
        exit()
